# Tooling

[P2] Existing manifests, lockfiles, tool configs, and CI scripts win.

## Packages

- Use the repo's package manager. New Python projects prefer `uv`; new Node tooling prefers local scripts and locked dependencies over global installs or unpinned `npx`.
- Before adding a dependency, read the manifest and lockfile and justify why existing dependencies are insufficient.

## Checks

- Prefer repo scripts (`make check`, `npm test`, `cargo test`, `go test`, `uv run`) or their CI equivalent.
- Run narrow checks first. Before finishing a change to shared behavior, public APIs, security, data, or build tooling, also run the repo's broad checks, including static security analysis, dependency audit, and secret scan where available.
- Report a missing tool as `not available`, never as passed.
- To find code only a retired entry point uses, remove the entry point and let the compiler's or language's dead-code analysis list the rest; hand-written call graphs miss macro, trait, and dynamic edges.

## Formatting

- The repo's config (`pyproject.toml`/`ruff.toml`, `.editorconfig`, `.golangci.yml`, `rustfmt.toml`, `eslint.config.*`, `.shellcheckrc`) is the single source of style for editor, pre-commit, and CI. Keep each setting in one place and pin the same tool version everywhere.
- Run formatters and linters through pre-commit (`pre-commit install` in each clone) so they gate every commit against the staged snapshot; never `--no-verify`.
- Format on save only with the gate's tool and config, and open each repo as its own editor root so the tool finds that repo's config. When editor output differs from the gate, fix the editor config once instead of hand-fixing files.
- Never lint or reformat generated code or test fixtures; exclude them in config.

## Git

- Commit atomically with imperative messages. Completing implementation does not imply publication: commit locally; push and PR/MR creation need explicit authorization, after which finish the whole publication workflow (remote verification, push, PR/MR, branch cleanup) without asking again.
- Preserve protected and default-branch policy unless the task or the repo's workflow selects that branch.
- A workflow is commit-only only when its command or the user says it packages an accepted change, as `ship-commit` does; `ship`, `publish`, `release`, `deploy`, or `distribute` alone do not make it so, and those workflows may edit what their own contracts require. A commit-only task reuses current review evidence or runs one bounded read-only review, makes only mechanical edits (staging, formatter, hook, metadata, signing, publication blockers), and never patches findings, starts a review-fix loop, or re-reviews.
- Report defects, design issues, and missing tests in chat; create no report or findings file unless asked. Findings block publication only when the user or repo defines the review as a blocking gate.
- If a hook blocks the commit because of a product defect, report the blocker and its unblock path; never bypass the hook or switch to development work without an explicit fix request.

## Environments

- Resolve the real environment and target before migrations or data mutation; use the narrowest operation and capture a rollback point when the system supports one.
- Prove changes in local, disposable, or staging environments first.
